floor inequality

    floor(m/p^(bâˆ’h)) >= p^h floor(m/p^b)

shows R_h(m)<=T_h(m)âˆ’m, hence 2R_h(m)<=W(m)âˆ’m.

On the other hand, setting u=floor(m/2), every difference floor(m/p^b)âˆ’2floor(u/p^b) is 0 or 1. Therefore

    W(m)âˆ’2W(u) <= 1+sum_(b=1)^d(p^bâˆ’p^(bâˆ’1))=p^d<=m.

For positive even m, the last inequality is strict because p^d is odd and m is even. Combining the two displays proves

    R_h(m)<=W(floor(m/2)),

strictly for every positive even m. This was derived independently of the route reviewer's digit-sum proof. The author's digit-sum proof was then audited separately and also passes: the identity for floor(m/2), superadditivity of W, and strictness from the positive even digit sum are all valid.

The complementary identity R_h(u)+R_h(nâˆ’1âˆ’u)=R_h(nâˆ’1) is exact, since n is divisible by each p^b occurring in the sum. The endpoint evaluation Bâˆ’R_h(nâˆ’1) equals nâˆ’beta for r even and ((p+1)/2)p^(râˆ’1)âˆ’beta for r odd. Both are <=nâˆ’beta. No ramification restriction enters these integer identities.

## 3. Full root covering, including the previously unresolved inner classes

This is a global argument on H_n, not an inference from outer residues. Suppose z lies outside every closed beta disk. Choose a maximizing delta=v(z+q^a), a in [0,nâˆ’1], so delta<beta. For j different from a, v(q^jâˆ’q^a)=p^ord_p(jâˆ’a). The distance v(z+q^j) is the minimum of this value and delta. When the values coincide, strict cancellation would create a value greater than the chosen maximum delta, so equality of the minimum still holds.

For a consecutive string of length l, cap the geometric distances at beta and count each residue class modulo p^b. The sum is at most beta+T_h(lâˆ’1). It is strictly smaller for the original distances: either the top class modulo p^h occurs and its actual distance is delta<beta, or that class is absent and its count is strictly below ceil(l/p^h). This handles delta<1 as well; all capped comparison values are then still valid upper bounds.

For k=nâˆ’2u<n odd, reflection gives W(n)âˆ’W(k)=n+W(2uâˆ’1). The k-th term of the Newton expansion minus the valuation of N_n is strictly greater than

    nâˆ’betaâˆ’W(u)+R_h(2uâˆ’1).

The complementary identity transforms the negative part to W(u)+R_h(nâˆ’2u)âˆ’R_h(nâˆ’1). Here nâˆ’2u is a positive odd integer <n. Apply the independently verified inequality to this integer and use superadditivity at u+((nâˆ’1)/2âˆ’u)=(nâˆ’1)/2. The displayed lower bound is nonnegative. Thus every other term is strictly larger than N_n, so H_n cannot vanish outside the disks.

This proof includes all roots, zero and all deeper inner classes, with multiplicity allowed at this stage. It neither assumes squarefreeness nor applies simple-root Hensel lifting to a repeated residual. The disks are correctly closed: the even-r endpoint bound can be exactly zero before the strict distance comparison. Centers differing by a multiple of p^h are at distance >=beta, so the beta representatives suffice. No counting or missing inner-class assumption is hidden in this reduction.

## 4. Unique double-summand dominance and cancellation audit

On z=âˆ’q^a+t^beta Y every z power is integral with weight zero. The consecutive-factor count gives wt(N_m)>=T_h(m). Hence the double summand has weight at least

    W(n)âˆ’[W((nâˆ’k)/2)+p^ord_p(kâˆ’m)+R_h(m)].

For m=0, fixed p-adic order is maximized at the smallest odd k, namely p^a0. The resulting function is B+g_a0, where g_a0=p^a0âˆ’a0(pâˆ’1)p^(a0âˆ’1)/2. Its first two increments are positive, and every subsequent increment is negative for p>=3. Thus (k,m)=(pÂ²,0) is uniquely best within m=0, with value B+p.

For positive even m, strict R_h(m)<W(m/2) and superadditivity reduce the bracket to a strict upper bound by the corresponding m=0 bracket for j=kâˆ’m. For odd m, j=kâˆ’m is positive even and j/2>=p^ord_p(j). Superadditivity implies the bracket is <=Bâˆ’W(j/2)+p^ord_p(j)<=B. Both cases are strictly below B+p. This exhausts every double-sum index.

The actual surviving term is L_n z^(pÂ²âˆ’1), not merely a lower bound. Its valuation is D and its residual relative to L_n is exactly 1, since z reduces to âˆ’1 and pÂ²âˆ’1 is even. Every competing term has an integer valuation gap >=1. Thus no cancellation can remove the leading unit. Evaluating the coefficientwise residual at any integral Y is legitimate and gives nonzero derivative of valuation D at each covered root.

## 5. Actual field, original P normalization, and consequences

The argument is in mixed characteristic, with e=v(p)=p^(sâˆ’1)(pâˆ’1), not in a characteristic-p truncation. The inequalities use exact values v(1âˆ’q^j)=p^ord_p(j), valid because 0<j<p^s. They remain valid even when D>=e.

The exact identity is H_n(vÂ²)=v^nP_n(v+v^(âˆ’1)). The original recurrence reduces P_n to X^n, so its roots reduce to zero and its values at Â±2 are units. The corresponding v values are units reducing to a square root of âˆ’1, not Â±1, so both coordinate maps have unit derivatives. This proves multiplicity and derivative-valuation transfer without silently multiplying by a nonunit.

The full differentiated identity is

    P_n'(X)=2v^(1âˆ’n)/(1âˆ’v^(âˆ’2)) H_n'(vÂ²)
             âˆ’n v^(âˆ’1)/(1âˆ’v^(âˆ’2)) P_n(X).

At roots the second term vanishes. On the entire local X disk it has valuation at least v(n)=r e>D, and the first prefactor reduces to (âˆ’1)^((nâˆ’1)/2). The implicit quadratic inverse near i exists over the integral valuation ring because p is odd and its derivative is a unit. Thus the author's stronger coefficientwise P-disk residual is also justified.

Both polynomials are monic of degree n. The product formula for the ordinary discriminant therefore gives nD for each. For the ordinary algebraic-square CD kernel, P_n and P_(nâˆ’1) cannot share a root: the nonzero original recurrence coefficients would propagate such a common zero back to P_0=1. Combined with the established derivative nonvanishing and nonzero w_(nâˆ’1), the exact CD identity proves every claimed kernel is nonzero. No positivity or absolute-value-square argument occurs, and no common valuation for all kernels is claimed.

## 6. Independent exact controls and attempted counterexamples

check_independent.py is fresh standard-library code and imports no author or previous reviewer implementation. INDEPENDENT_CHECKS.json and CHECK_RUN.log record the following:

- Three rational q specializations of the exact Newton derivative and original H/P Laurent identities through degree 11.
- Randomized and boundary integer checks for all six primes 3,5,7,11,17,31 and every r from 2 through 17. The seed is fixed. All key digit, complementary-tail, root-cover, and double-summand inequalities pass.
- Exhaustive double-sum enumeration in eleven grids, including n=729 and n=625. Every grid has precisely the winner (pÂ²,0), with a positive gap to the runner-up.
- Exact characteristic-zero original-H coefficient reconstruction in Z[q]/Phi_(p^s), followed by DIRECT ordinary-derivative Taylor translation at 26 centers. Cases are (p,r,s)=(3,2,3),(3,3,4),(3,3,5),(3,4,5),(3,5,6),(5,3,4). All beta centers are tested where modest, with centers 0,1,8 tested for (3,5,6).

Unlike a modulo-p truncated series check, the actual-field implementation reduces to a degree-<e cyclotomic representative and then uses an independent Horner q=1+t conversion. The weights e ord_p(c_j)+j are mutually distinct modulo e, so their minimum is exact, including above ramification. The leading-unit conversion p/t^e -> âˆ’1 is retained. The code differentiates H before translation, rather than deriving ordinary derivatives from residue polynomials. It verifies constant weight D and the exact leading unit of L_n, and strictly larger weights for every nonconstant local derivative coefficient.

In particular, (3,4,5) passes with D=186>e=162 on all nine disks, and (3,5,6) passes with D=645>e=486. The larger-s case separately checks the lack of dependence on the minimal ramification value. No numerical root solver or floating-point approximation is used. These finite controls could have exposed errors but are not the source of the unlimited theorem.

## 7. Frozen-byte verdict

**PASS**, with no requested mathematical revision, in the exact pure-power scope stated above.

The accepted final author report SHA-256 is

    a8922486de970fcca8f1ccaf15b384b5a561b0ff2a8d650a6d2447051121428c

and its manifest SHA-256 is

    9c9d45e2ce44ce14904ce68e59f6b72246980f7d57c7a444ce86274cfbc40341.

All 11 author-manifest files, the exact file inventory, the manifest seal, and retained-source equality to the original local files were verified. The complete author package is retained under sources/author-final. The author checker was replayed in an isolated temporary directory; every recorded mathematical value agreed exactly after removing elapsed-time fields. Original author files were checked again and remained unchanged. CANDIDATE_VERIFICATION.json and AUTHOR_REPLAY.log record this verification.

This acceptance newly certifies the all-root squarefreeness, common original ordinary derivative valuation, both discriminant valuations, and all original algebraic-square kernel nonvanishing at every pure-power degree n=p^r with odd p, r>=2, s>r. It is not restricted to outer roots, the distinguished zero root, bounded r, or below-ramification calculations. It does not assert arbitrary-degree or arbitrary-odd-composite-order closure. Earlier artifacts were not rewritten.
