PK␀␀␀␀␀␀␀!␀Áé'Z␀␀'Z␀␀T␀␀␀f020-even-leading-odd-tail-public-20261021_v1/author/sources/c2-odd-author/REPORT.md# Uniform second correction for the simultaneous odd-tail obstructions

Author candidate, 21 October 2026 UTC. This is a proof in the ORIGINAL F020 normalization. It does not accept itself. The result is a uniform lift, not an enumeration of additional primes, an iff, an arbitrary-digit/global F020/F023 closure, an algorithm substitution, an FFT/cost assertion, or a historical novelty claim.

## 1. Exact result and inherited boundary

Let p>=5 be prime, r>=2, s>r, d=2m+1 odd with 1<=d<p, N=p^r, n=2N+d, and q have exact characteristic-zero order p^s. Keep exactly

    P_0=1, P_1=X, P_(j+1)=X P_j-(1-q^j)P_(j-1),
    H_j(z)=sum_a [j choose a]_q z^a.

Choose an algebraic p-adic embedding, put t=q-1, normalize v(t)=1, and set

    e=v(p)=(p-1)p^(s-1), B=r(p-1)p^(r-1)/2,
    K=N+2B, E=N/2+2B, D=3N/2+2B-p,
    C_m=(2m+1)!!, B_m=(2m)!!, alpha_m=B_m/C_m.

THE UNIFORM LIFT. Suppose r=2, p divides C_m-B_m, and p=2d+1=4m+3. Necessarily p>=7. Put delta=p(p-1)/2, rho=p/2, and X^2=t^p U. All 2p central roots left open by the accepted odd-tail criterion satisfy

    v(U+1)>2,
    v(P_n'(X))=K+delta+m+2,
    P_n'(X)/t^(K+delta+m+2) -> C_m/160 !=0 in F_p.      (1.1)

Here and throughout, prime on a polynomial denotes its ORIGINAL ordinary characteristic-zero derivative. The root count includes multiplicity until (1.1) proves simplicity. No exact deeper root depth is asserted.

COROLLARY WITH THE ACCEPTED CRITERION. For EVERY p>=5, r>=2, s>r and odd d<p in this leading-two sector, original P_n and H_n are squarefree and the original algebraic-square Christoffel-Darboux kernel is nonzero at every P_n root. There are no remaining simultaneous obstructions within this stated sector. The accepted criterion already resolves all other cases. This is not a theorem for arbitrary n, other leading digits, even tails, s<=r, or composite orders.

The only allowed odd digit at p=3 is d=1. This case is separately inherited: accepted general leading-digit coverage for r>=3, and accepted original P_19 for r=2, whose central ordinary derivative value is 76/3. The present second-order argument does NOT apply to p=3 or rederive that result. At p=5 a simultaneous constraint p=2d+1 would force even d=2, so there is no missing small-prime lift. At p=2 the leading-two premise 2<p fails and the minimal-s growth check fails; no p=2 assertion is made.

For the first simultaneous case p=23,d=11, (1.1) gives value1801 and unit1, recovering the accepted bounded result. That case is inherited evidence, not an additional new theorem credited here.

## 2. Exact source bindings and all-root facts used

Four accepted packages are retained byte for byte under dependencies, pinned by the SHA-256 of MANIFEST.json:

    odd-tail-author: 3d4cce4aa5586a9f4ad82225d183f22d8aed3616b65b6fa34b8b0aab20aad565
    odd-tail-review: fb9b967054ded8ed059d51dc460aded01cc3c93ffd47b7917ae3614d590ce18d
    first-exception-author: f39d8151a03665b72664b8e56db811bbd12088c66c5025e930cb5287a2c260c6
    first-exception-review: 0b8b9e0658d04dd3e24ea1baf58a5ff6595d6738f1c64a06cc74f4112a8a28c7

The first pair supplies the original coefficientwise Newton estimates, the exact odd-tail transfer, the complete root partition, and the already resolved derivative cases. The second pair is a verified p=23 comparison and supplies the bounded proof mechanism; its numerical margins and coefficients are not extrapolated to other primes. All needed uniform replacements are proved below. Reviews are bound to their exact authors; no unaccepted +5 result is assumed.

For the new work r=2, N=p^2, B=p(p-1), delta=B/2, rho=p/2, g=(p-1)/2. Put

    A=P_(2N), M=P_(2N-1), a=1-q^(2N).

On the central disk X=mu Y, mu^2=t^p, the accepted coefficientwise facts are

    weight(A)=K, A(mu Y)/t^K -> (Y^2+1)^p,
    weight(M)=E, M(mu Y)/(t^(2B)mu^p) -> epsilon Y^p,
    weight(A')=D, A'(mu Y)/(t^(3B)mu^p) -> -2 epsilon Y^p,
    weight(M')>=E, a/t^N -> -2,
    epsilon=(-1)^((p-1)/2).                              (2.1)

At every central point with residual Y^2=-1, M and A' have EXACT values E,D. These estimates concern whole disks or residual loci, not roots of a previously studied polynomial.

Use the exact original Newton products

    w_b=product_(j=1)^b(1-q^j), Q_b=product_(j=1)^b(1-q^(2j)),
    N_b(z)=product_(j=0)^(b-1)(z+q^j).

The original H and ordinary H' identities are

    H_b=sum_(k=b mod 2) w_b N_k/[w_k Q_((b-k)/2)],
    H_b'=sum_(k=b mod 2,k>=1)sum_(0<=u<k)
      w_b z^(k-u-1)N_u/[Q_((b-k)/2)w_u(1-q^(k-u))].       (2.2)

On the central boundary the two H_(2N) pivots are N_(2N) and C0=w_(2N)/Q_N. Every other even term has relative gap at least delta+g. The unique M-side H pivot and ordinary derivative pivot are

    S_M=w_(2N-1)N_N/[w_N Q_((N-1)/2)],
    S_D=w_(2N)z^(N-1)N_N/[w_N(1-q^N)].                  (2.3)

Every other term in the corresponding sums has relative gap at least g. The accepted gap proof uses, for j=lN+ap+b, 0<=a,b<p,

    T(j)=j+(rho-1)ceil(j/p), W(j)=sum_(u=1)^j p^ord_p(u),
    2[T(j)-W(j)/2]=a(p-1)+b+(p-2)1_(b>0),

and for derivative terms the excess bound

    p+W(j-1)/2-p^ord_p(j)+[T(u)-W(u)/2], j=k-u.

Only j=u=N gives zero derivative slack. This keeps every ordinary derivative competitor and gives g>2 for p>=7. The ordinary M' estimate in (2.1) is separately proved in the accepted sources; it is never obtained by differentiating a Frobenius residual.

## 3. Exact tail, full derivative, and root counts

For b_j=1-q^(2N+j), define

    f_0=1, f_1=X, f_(j+1)=X f_j-b_j f_(j-1),
    g_0=0, g_1=1, g_(j+1)=X g_j-b_j g_(j-1).

The exact original recurrence gives P_(2N+j)=f_j A-a g_j M. For d=2m+1 set

    T_m=f_d/X, V_m=g_d, G=M/X, kappa=V_m/T_m,
    F=P_n/X=T_m A-a V_m G=T_m J, J=A-a kappa G.          (3.1)

All of T_m,V_m,G are polynomials in X^2. The continuants have exact matching descriptions

    T_m=sum_(S nonadjacent subset {1,...,2m})
           (-1)^|S| X^(2m-2|S|) product_(j in S)b_j,
    V_m=sum_(S nonadjacent subset {2,...,2m})
           (-1)^|S| X^(2m-2|S|) product_(j in S)b_j.      (3.2)

On a high disk, T_m/t^m and V_m/t^m have respective unit constants C_m,B_m. Their coefficients as polynomials in W=X^2/t are integral. Thus T_m/t^m is an analytic unit on every high disk, kappa is analytic, and

    v(kappa)=0, v(kappa')>=x-1 when v(X)=x>=1.           (3.3)

The accepted count follows coefficientwise from weight(a V_m G)>=K+m+delta>K+m centrally, and its analogous strict bound on the other disks: the residual high-root polynomial is unchanged, with multiplicities retained. In the r=2 case there are p disjoint disks centered at x_a=i(q^(a/2)-q^(-a/2)), 0<=a<p, with v(X-x_a)>=rho. Each contains exactly 2p boundary roots. The p-1 noncentral disks have v(X)=1; the central disk has v(X)=rho. In addition there are one exact zero and d-1 low roots of value1/2. The count

    1+(d-1)+(p-1)2p+2p=2p^2+d

exhausts the degree before any simplicity argument. This count is Weierstrass/Newton counting, not simple-root Hensel applied to an inseparable high residual.

Differentiate the whole J in characteristic zero:

    J'=A'-a kappa M'/X+a kappa M/X^2-a kappa' M/X,
    Qstar=X^2 A'/(aM)+kappa-kappa X M'/M-X kappa',
    J'=(aM/X^2)Qstar.                                   (3.4)

At a nonzero root, J=0 exactly, hence

    P_n'=X T_m J'.                                      (3.5)

The four terms of J' have values/lower bounds D, N+E-x, N+E-2x, N+E-1. At a noncentral root the first is uniquely lowest, giving v(P_n')=D+m+1. At a central point with residual U=-1, the last two bracket terms of Qstar have values at least rho and p-1, both >2 for p>=7. They are retained and estimated, not dropped without a bound.

All recurrence edges and imported q-factorial denominators are nonzero, because

    2p^r+d <=2p^r+p-2 <p^(r+1)<=p^s.

## 4. Uniform original scalar cancellation beyond 2p

We prove for every p>=7 and s>2

    v(A(X))>K+2p at X^2=-t^p.                           (4.1)

First work formally in characteristic p, using the same polynomial identities. Let z=v0^2, X=v0+v0^(-1), with v0 near i, and S=z+1=v0 X. Then S^2=zX^2, v(S)=rho. Define

    R0=product_(j=1)^(p-1)(q^j-1),
    D0=product_(1<=j<2p, j odd, j!=p)(1-q^j),
    Rhat=R0/t^(p-1), Dhat=D0/t^(p-1).

Both index sets reduce bijectively to F_p^*. Binomial coefficients through degree3 depend only on the residue of the exponent, since 2!,3! are units. There are p-1 factors, so the minus signs in D0 cancel. Consequently

    Rhat=Dhat=-[1+t/2-t^2/12]+O(t^3).                   (4.2)

For an explicit derivation, a normalized edge has coefficients u_j=(j-1)/2 and v_j=(j-1)(j-2)/6. Over all nonzero residues, sum u_j=1/2, sum v_j=-1/3, sum u_j^2=-1/4. The cross coefficient is ((1/2)^2+1/4)/2=1/4, giving -1/12 after adding sum v_j. Wilson gives leading product -1. This is a uniform finite-product identity, not a fitted jet.

For any integral Z,C the exact characteristic-p polynomial

    product_(a=0)^(p-1)(Z+C(1+h)^a)-(Z+C)^p

is divisible by h^(p-1). Indeed the intermediate Gaussian-binomial coefficients have h-order p-1: in [p choose k]_(1+h), 1<=k<p, numerator 1-(1+h)^p=-h^p has order p while the corresponding denominator 1-(1+h)^k has order1; all other factors have equal numerator/denominator orders. The extreme coefficient correction has order at least p. Therefore grouping N_N by indices j=b+pa gives

    N_N=N_p^p(1+error of value >=p(p-2)/2).              (4.3)

In the zero-residue group the absolute error has value at least p(p-1), while (z+1)^p has value p*rho=p^2/2. Other groups have leading value p and relative gap at least p(p-2). Thus (4.3) holds also at the scalar points in question.

Since C0 is the product over odd indices 1<=j<2p^2, group j=b+2pa with b odd in 1..2p-1. For b!=p the same argument, now h=q^(2p)-1 of value p, has relative gap p(p-2). The group b=p is EXACTLY [(1-q^p)D0]^p. Consequently

    C0=-t^N D0^(2p)(1+error of value >=p(p-2)).          (4.4)

Write N_p=S R, R=product_(j=1)^(p-1)(z+q^j). Each nonzero-residue factor differs from its R0 counterpart by S, so v(R/R0-1)>=rho-1. At X^2=-t^p,

    N_p^2-t^p D0^2=-t^p(z R^2+D0^2).

After normalization by t^(p+2(p-1)), the bracket has value at least

    min(3,rho-1,rho)>2,                                (4.5)

by (4.2), since p>=7. Raising to the pth power, with the exact characteristic-p Frobenius identity, gives relative value >2p. Both grouping errors are also >2p:

    p(p-2)/2>2p, p(p-2)>2p for p>=7.

Further, N_(2N)=N_N^2(1+error of value >=N-rho), by the shift q^N-1=t^N in the second block; N-rho>2p. Every nonpivot in H_(2N) has relative gap delta+g=(p^2-1)/2>2p. Thus N_(2N)+C0 and all omitted terms prove (4.1) in characteristic p. The H/P coordinate prefactor is a unit.

TRANSFER TO THE ORIGINAL INTEGER POLYNOMIAL. A coefficient of X^(2l) in A=P_(2N) is a sum of products of N-l recurrence edges. After X^2=-t^p, each raw t monomial has degree at least N+(p-1)l>=N. Every coefficient discarded modulo p therefore has actual value at least e+N. Uniformly for every s>2,

    e>=(p-1)p^2=pB,
    e+N-K>=pB-2B=(p-2)B>2p.                           (4.6)

The characteristic-p calculation excludes all non-p-divisible coefficients through degree K+2p. Together with the raw lattice bound (4.6), it proves (4.1) in the original normalization for every primitive phase. No unsupported shortcut e>K+2p and no fixed-s extrapolation is used.

## 5. Root feedback on every central branch: J, never F

Write J(mu Y)=t^K j(U), U=Y^2. On the full closed U-unit disk, j is integral restricted analytic: A/t^K is integral, a kappa G/t^K has weight at least delta, and T_m/t^m is an analytic unit. The residual is (U+1)^p.

The whole-disk ORIGINAL J' has weight at least D. A' has this bound. Polynomial division and ordinary differentiation give weight(G')>=E-2rho, hence weight(a kappa G')>=N+E-p=D; the other differentiated term has weight at least N+E-1>D by (3.3). The exact chain rule

    J'(mu Y)=2t^K mu^(-1)Y j_U(U)

therefore gives coefficientwise weight(j_U)>=D-K+rho=delta. Division by Y is legal since J is even. This strong bound is NOT assigned to F=T_m J: differentiating F on the whole disk introduces the extra T_m' J term.

By (4.1) and delta>2p, v(j(-1))>2p. Translate j(-1+V)=sum_l h_l V^l. Translation preserves integrality and the derivative bound. Its residual is V^p, so h_p is a unit. For 1<=l<p, l is a p-adic unit, whence v(h_l)>=delta>2p; also v(h_0)>2p. If a new central root had 0<v(V)<=2, the term h_p V^p would have value <=2p, strictly below the constant and all terms l<p, and below every term l>p by integrality and v(V)>0. This unique minimum cannot sum to zero, including for a restricted analytic series. Thus EVERY one of the 2p central roots, with all branches and multiplicities retained, satisfies

    v(U+1)>2.                                         (5.1)

This proves precisely the root feedback needed through order two. A scalar derivative at U=-1 is not used to assert a derivative at a root.

## 6. Uniform ordinary Newton-pivot quotient to second order

Pairing the single ordinary derivative pivot S_D and single M pivot S_M in (2.3), and retaining the original coordinate identity, yields on the entire residual locus U=-1

    X^2 A'/(aM)=X^2 [2z^N/(z-1)] Q_((N-1)/2)/(1-q^N)
                       +error of value >=g.             (6.1)

This imported exact pivot reduction is justified coefficientwise, not by an old root equation. Both pivots are nonzero there, and g>2. The coordinate-prefactor ordinary derivative term involving 2N A has relative value at least 2e+K-D>2. The scalar 2z^N/(z-1)=1+error of value >=rho>2, since z=-1+error rho and N is odd. Also (1-q^N)/(-t^N)=1+error>2; discarded integer coefficients have relative bound e+1-N>2.

Put g=(p-1)/2 and A0=(p^2-1)/2=pg+g. In Q_A0, the g indices divisible by p have no positive normalized characteristic-p coefficient below t^p. All others have normalized first and second coefficients

    u_j=(2j-1)/2=j-1/2,
    v_j=(2j-1)(2j-2)/6=(2/3)j^2-j+1/3.

The unit-index multiset is g copies of residues 1,...,p-1 followed by 1,...,g. Using g=-1/2 in F_p and the elementary sums of first and second powers gives

    sum u_j=-1/8, sum v_j=1/8, sum u_j^2=1/8.           (6.2)

For example, each complete residue block contributes 1/2,-1/3,-1/4 respectively; the partial block contributes 1/8,-1/24,0. Multiplication by g=-1/2 gives (6.2). The second product coefficient is

    1/8+[(1/64)-(1/8)]/2=9/128.

The leading product is -1: (-2)^A0=1 modulo p, while the remaining products give (-1)^g(g!)^2=-1 by Wilson. Consequently

    Q_A0/(-t^B)=1-t/8+9t^2/128+error>2.                (6.3)

For a p-divisible edge the discarded integer coefficients have normalized value at least e+1-p>2, and for a unit edge at least e>2. There is no edge divisible by p^2 in Q_A0. These bounds control the entire mixed-characteristic product, uniformly for every s>2. Equations (6.1)-(6.3) therefore prove at every central boundary point with residual U=-1

    X^2 A'/(aM)=U[1-t/8+9t^2/128]+error>2.

Only now apply the all-root depth (5.1), obtaining at EVERY new central root

    X^2 A'/(aM)=-1+t/8-9t^2/128+error>2.               (6.4)

The U+1 displacement has been proved irrelevant at this precision, not silently set to zero.

## 7. Uniform second matching moment and the exact kappa jet

For j<=2m<p, through relative order two on the central boundary,

    -b_j/t=j[1+(j-1)t/2+(j-1)(j-2)t^2/6]+error>2.      (7.1)

Modulo p, q^(2N+j)=(1+t^N)^2(1+t)^j; the shift first contributes at relative degree N-1>2. Discarded integer coefficients after division by t have value at least e>2. Every non-maximal matching in normalized T_m or V_m includes a positive power of W=X^2/t and hence has value at least p-1>2. Thus (3.2), not an approximate tail recurrence, reduces the desired jet to maximal matchings.

V_m has the unique maximal matching {2,4,...,2m}. T_m has S_l={1,3,...,2l-1} union {2l+2,2l+4,...,2m}, 0<=l<=m, with weights

    w_l=(2l-1)!!(2m)!!/(2l)!!, sum_l w_l=C_m.

Put a_l=(2l-1)!!/(2l)!!. Its recurrence a_l/a_(l-1)=(2l-1)/(2l) proves, by induction in m,

    sum_l a_l=(2m+1)a_m,
    sum_l l a_l=m(2m+1)a_m/3,
    sum_l l(l-1)a_l=m(m-1)(2m+1)a_m/5.

The formulas include m=0,1, with empty terms zero. Hence the weighted means are

    mean(l)=m/3, mean(l^2)=m(3m+2)/15.                  (7.2)

All denominators are p-units for the new p>=7 range. Let u_j=(j-1)/2, v_j=(j-1)(j-2)/6. The log-second coefficient v_j-u_j^2/2=(j-1)(j-5)/24 is only a finite algebraic device. For the even matching its sum is m(4m^2-12m-1)/72, while its first coefficient is m^2/2. Replacing even2k by odd2k-1 for k<=l changes the first sum by -l/2 and the log-second sum by (-2l^2+5l)/24. Therefore, if E2 denotes the even matching's second coefficient, S_l has

    first coefficient (m^2-l)/2,
    second coefficient E2+[(5-6m^2)l+l^2]/24.

Averaging with (7.2) gives the exact uniform jets

    V_m/t^m=B_m[1+m^2 t/2
                +m(m-1)(9m^2+13m+1)t^2/72]+error>2,
    T_m/t^m=C_m[1+m(3m-1)t/6
                +m(m-1)(45m^2+35m-22)t^2/360]+error>2.

Formal division by the analytic unit T_m/t^m now gives

    kappa=alpha_m[1+(m/6)t+m(7m-27)t^2/360]+error>2.     (7.3)

This formula retains the full continuant ratio. It is proved for all m with d<p and p>=7, not inferred from sample digits.

## 8. The complete second correction is universally nonzero

Insert (6.4) and (7.3) into the ENTIRE bracket (3.4). The terms kappa X M'/M and X kappa' have respective values at least rho>2 and p-1>2. All nonpivot, coordinate, mixed-characteristic, and root-displacement errors have already been bounded strictly beyond two. Therefore at every new central root

    Qstar=(alpha_m-1)+(1/8+alpha_m m/6)t
              +[alpha_m m(7m-27)/360-9/128]t^2+error>2.

Under the simultaneous constraints, alpha_m=1 modulo p and m=-3/4 modulo p. The constant and linear coefficients have actual value at least e (the constant may be exactly zero), since their rational denominators are p-units; their contributions are strictly beyond two. The second coefficient is

    m(7m-27)/360-9/128
        =43/640-45/640=-1/320 in F_p.                   (8.1)

This is a unit for EVERY p>=7. Thus no third cancellation remains under the simultaneous constraints in the accepted criterion's p>=5 scope. The conclusion is uniform, not based on finding or not finding more exceptional primes.

Since v(aM/X^2)=D, (8.1) gives v(J')=D+2. Using (3.5), v(T_m)=m, and rho+D=K+delta proves the value in (1.1). For the normalized original P unit, (2.1) gives

    [X T_m (aM/X^2)]/t^(K+delta+m)
         -> -2 C_m epsilon Y^(p-1)=-2 C_m,

because Y^2=-1 and epsilon^2=1. Multiplication by -1/320 yields C_m/160, a unit. This applies to all central roots, not only the scalar point U=-1.

## 9. Every root, original H, and original algebraic-square kernel

The accepted odd-tail theorem supplies, in general r>=2, one exact zero, d-1 simple low roots, and exactly 2p^r high roots in its disjoint disks. Their exact formulas remain unchanged. For completeness, put h=floor(r/2), beta=p^h, L=p^r/beta, and rho=beta/2 if r is even, or rho=p^(h-1)(2p-1)/2 if r is odd. Each disk centered at x_a=i(q^(a/2)-q^(-a/2)), 0<=a<beta, contains 2L boundary roots. The zero has derivative value K+m. Each low root has derivative value N+m and normalized original derivative unit

    d Z^(2N) M_(d-1)(Z),
    M_j(Z)=j! sum_k Z^(j-2k)/(2^k k!(j-2k)!), X=t^(1/2)Z.

The low residue M_d is separable: M_d'=d M_(d-1), and a common root propagates backward through the recurrence M_(j+1)=Z M_j+j M_(j-1) to M_0=1, since 1,...,d are units. This ordinary derivative identity is checked from the original differentiated recurrence, not differentiation of its inseparable high factor.

Outside r=2 central ties, a high root with x=v(X) has

    v(P_n')=x+m+min(D,N+E-2x).

At r=2 central roots, the accepted first two cases are

    p not dividing C_m-B_m: value K+delta+m, unit 2(C_m-B_m);
    p dividing C_m-B_m but not 2d+1:
         value K+delta+m+1, unit -C_m(2d+1)/12.

The new third case (1.1) completes this list. Thus every root of every parameter in Section1's sector is covered, with zero, low, noncentral, and central roots all accounted for. The p=3,d=1 cases remain separately inherited as stated there.

Keep the exact identities

    H_n(v0^2)=v0^n P_n(X), X=v0+v0^(-1),
    H_n'(z)=v0^(n-2)[(n/2)P_n+(v0-v0^(-1))P_n'/2].

All P roots are small and not +/-2; all H roots are near -1 and nonzero. The branch near i is an analytic isometry covering all roots, preserving multiplicity and derivative valuation. At a root the P_n prefactor term is exactly zero. The derivative multiplier has residue i^(n-1)=(-1)^(m+1). Hence H has the same derivative values and its new central unit is (-1)^(m+1) C_m/160. In particular it is squarefree. Equality of H/P units is not silently assumed for general m.

Every recurrence edge 1-q^j, 1<=j<n, is nonzero by the growth check. A common root of P_n,P_(n-1) would propagate backwards to P_0=1. Therefore at any P_n root xi the ORIGINAL identity

    sum_(j=0)^(n-1) P_j(xi)^2/w_j
       =P_n'(xi)P_(n-1)(xi)/w_(n-1)

is nonzero. These are algebraic squares, not absolute-value squares, and no positivity or real interlacing is invoked. All integer lattices, mixed-characteristic margins and residue units hold for every s>r and every primitive phase. Injectivity of algebraic embeddings and Galois conjugacy transfer nonvanishing to every characteristic-zero primitive phase.

## 10. Controls, precise stopping point, and review boundary

The proof is Sections2-9, not the finite controls. The reproducible checker independently multiplies finite edge jets, checks matching moments and exact tail identities, evolves original parity recurrences and their ordinary U derivatives at diagnostic scalar points, verifies all root counts and precision competitors, and falsifies boundary shortcuts. Polynomial inequalities are recorded as nonnegative-coefficient polynomials in p-7, proving their uniform range rather than only checking a finite prime list.

The scalar original recurrence is a diagnostic only: at p=23,d=11 it again has scalar P' starting at value1799/unit19, while the rootwise derivative has value1801/unit1. The strong whole-disk bound is on J_U, not F_U; the first-exception accepted evidence gives the latter a nonzero earlier term. The controls retain these distinctions. No original scalar derivative is substituted for an all-root argument.

The complete inventory and source pins are frozen, with isolated resource-capped cold replay and negative inventory tests. A fresh independent reviewer must verify the exact bytes, all-p/all-s estimates, scalar transfer, whole-disk J derivative, matching moments, full ordinary derivative bracket, inherited small primes, exhaustive root partition, and original H/kernel transfer. No native document, GitHub repository, Library item, accepted source, or pending table-union package is modified. The sole deliverable is this local author candidate and its reviewable controls.
